{"question": "Write a C++ function `std::vector<int> mergeSorted(const std::vector<int> &a, const std::vector<int> &b)` that merges two already-sorted vectors into one sorted vector.", "starter_code": "std::vector<int> mergeSorted(const std::vector<int> &a, const std::vector<int> &b) {
    // TODO
}", "answer": "std::vector<int> mergeSorted(const std::vector<int> &a, const std::vector<int> &b) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    size_t i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {
        if (a[i] <= b[j]) result.push_back(a[i++]);
        else result.push_back(b[j++]);
    }
    while (i < a.size()) result.push_back(a[i++]);
    while (j < b.size()) result.push_back(b[j++]);
    return result;
}", "explanation": "This is the merge step of merge sort: two pointers walk both sorted vectors simultaneously, always appending the smaller of the two current elements to the result, then appending any remaining tail elements once one vector is exhausted.", "concepts": ["merge sorted arrays", "two pointers", "merge sort"]}

